From 978132d982b3c2107ee78beaa2644c11d232c715 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maxime <maxime.lefrancois@emse.fr>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 23:31:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Natural datatypes for JSON values in RML-Core mappings

RML-Core gives a reference its natural RDF literal: a JSON number or
boolean is typed xsd:integer, xsd:double or xsd:boolean. The test cases of
the W3C KG-Construct CG expect it since kg-construct/rml-core#218 and the
RML-IO updates of 2025-08 to 2026-08 (e.g. RMLTC0002a, 0007c-f, 0008a-b,
0011b, 0012a of rml-core; RMLSTC0001a-b, 0002a-e, 0007a, 0008a-b and the
targets RMLTTC0000 to 0007a of rml-io). They were simple literals.

The Executor records whether the mapping is written in the vocabulary of
the CG before it is conformed; only then does the LiteralGenerator of an
object map with a reference and neither datatype nor language type the
values by their Java type (Boolean, integral Number, other Number; the
double in canonical form). Mappings in the legacy vocabulary keep simple
literals, as RMLio/rml-test-cases expect.

Tests: NaturalDatatypeTest (a CG mapping and a legacy one); the copies of
the CG cases whose expected outputs differed from the current suites only
by these datatypes are updated (9 rml-core cases, 39 rml-io files).
